---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ActionLabel from '../../components/ActionLabel.astro';
import '../../styles/guides-page.css';

const title = 'Your cloud bill grew. What changed?';
const description = 'Review your own cloud billing export with Cloud Cost Guard. Check supported AWS, Azure, and Google Cloud formats, understand the result, and choose what to investigate next.';
const articleUrl = 'https://cloudandcapital.com/insights/using-cloud-cost-guard';
const articleData = {
  '@type': 'Article',
  '@id': `${articleUrl}#article`,
  headline: title,
  description,
  author: { '@id': 'https://cloudandcapital.com/#diana' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://cloudandcapital.com/#organization' },
  mainEntityOfPage: articleUrl,
  datePublished: '2026-10-23',
  dateModified: '2026-10-23',
  image: 'https://cloudandcapital.com/images/cloud-cost-guard-billing-preview.jpg',
};
---
<BaseLayout title={`${title} | Cloud & Capital`} description={description} type="article" structuredData={[articleData]}>
  <main id="main-content" class="guides-page guard-guide">
    <article class="guide-shell guide-article">
      <header>
        <a class="guide-back" href="/insights">← Guides &amp; writing</a>
        <p class="guide-eyebrow">Cloud bills · A tool walkthrough</p>
        <h1><span>Your cloud bill grew.</span> <em>What changed?</em></h1>
        <p class="guide-byline">By Diana Molski · <time datetime="2026-10-23">October 23, 2026</time></p>
        <p class="guide-lede">A bigger bill tells you something changed. It doesn’t tell you where to start. Cloud Cost Guard breaks down your billing records and compares periods, so you can see which services deserve a closer look.</p>
        <p>Start with your own billing export. Upload one period for a spending breakdown, or add an earlier period to see what changed.</p>
        <a class="guide-button" href="https://guard.cloudandcapital.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-analytics-event="cta_click" data-analytics-destination="cloud_cost_guard" data-analytics-source="writing_archive"><ActionLabel label="Open Cloud Cost Guard" /></a>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="upload-title">
        <h2 id="upload-title">Start with your own bill.</h2>
        <p>Use a billing CSV for the period you want to review. Guard reads supported FOCUS exports, Google’s Cost table CSV, or its simple template. FOCUS is a shared format for billing records. Here’s the route for each provider.</p>
        <dl class="guard-providers">
          <div>
            <dt>AWS</dt>
            <dd>Create a <strong>FOCUS</strong> export in Billing and Cost Management → Data Exports. Download its CSV or <strong>.csv.gz</strong> files from S3 and select the parts together. Guard opens Gzip CSV files for you. <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cur/latest/userguide/dataexports-create-standard.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AWS export instructions</a>.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Azure</dt>
            <dd>In Cost Management → Exports, choose <strong>Cost and usage details (FOCUS)</strong>, where available for your billing account. Download the CSV or <strong>.csv.gz</strong> files from Azure storage. <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-improved-exports" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Azure export instructions</a>.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Google Cloud</dt>
            <dd>Open <strong>Billing → Cost table</strong>, choose your invoice month, reset filters and set <strong>Group by → No grouping</strong>. Include <strong>Service description, Cost type, and Unrounded cost</strong> in the columns, then download CSV. Choose it in Guard and confirm the invoice month and billing currency. English column names are supported. <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/cost-table" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google’s Cost table instructions</a>.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p>Open <strong>What can I upload?</strong> on Guard’s start screen for the template and format details. PDF invoices, screenshots, Excel files, ZIP archives, and Parquet files aren’t read directly.</p>
        <ol class="guard-steps">
          <li>Under <strong>Your current bill</strong>, select all CSV parts from one export run.</li>
          <li>For a Google Cost table, confirm the invoice month and three-letter currency code from your bill. Leave its invoice or statement header lines in the file. No template editing needed.</li>
          <li>To compare, add a separate earlier period under <strong>An earlier bill</strong>. This is optional.</li>
          <li>Choose <strong>Review my bill</strong>. The first review prepares the analysis tools on your device, so give it a moment.</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="guard-file-help">
          <summary>File requirements and limits</summary>
          <ul>
            <li>Each period allows up to <strong>20 CSV or Gzip CSV files, 10 MB combined before and after decompression, and 50,000 rows</strong>.</li>
            <li>The simple template needs <strong>Date, Service, Cost, and Currency</strong>. Preserve credits as negative amounts, and keep taxes and other charges you want included.</li>
            <li>Cost values keep up to <strong>18 decimal places</strong> through the analysis and downloads. Keep your original amounts; don’t round records just to force an upload.</li>
            <li>If timestamps leave out the timezone, Guard asks you to confirm UTC. Confirm only if your export uses UTC; otherwise export explicit UTC timestamps. The report records your choice.</li>
            <li>UTC timestamps can use minutes or seconds, such as <strong>2026-09-01T00:00Z</strong>. You don’t need to edit valid timestamps just to add seconds.</li>
            <li>FOCUS files need <strong>ChargePeriodStart, ChargePeriodEnd, ServiceName, BilledCost, and BillingCurrency</strong>. This is support for those columns, not validation of the full FOCUS specification. Rows with an empty service name keep their amounts and get a flagged “service not supplied” label.</li>
            <li>Google Cost table credits and taxes stay in the review. A Total row is checked separately and isn’t counted as another charge. Unrounded cost is preferred; the report flags any rounded Cost values it uses.</li>
            <li>Use one currency and consistent account coverage and cost basis. The comparison must be earlier and must not overlap the current period.</li>
            <li>If identical rows are flagged, check whether they’re repeated exports or separate charges before choosing how to count them. Don’t combine repeated month-to-date snapshots.</li>
          </ul>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="result-title">
        <h2 id="result-title">What does the result tell you?</h2>
        <p><strong>Net cloud cost</strong> shows the total from your supplied records, including signed credits. <strong>Services</strong> breaks it down by service. If you added an earlier period, <strong>Findings</strong> shows the biggest movements between the two uploads.</p>
        <p>Check the dates, currency, and included accounts before using the result. Different-length periods compare total spending, not daily spending rates. A partial export gives you a partial picture.</p>
        <p>Google’s Cost table groups activity by invoice month, which can include late-arriving usage from another month. Guard shows an invoice-month view for this export. Use a detailed daily FOCUS export if you want to explore daily changes.</p>
        <aside class="guide-example"><p><strong>“Service totals add up” checks the calculation.</strong> It doesn’t prove every account or charge was included. Compare the upload’s coverage and total with your provider bill.</p></aside>
        <p>The report covers the cloud records you supply. Separate software invoices, direct AI bills, or Kubernetes utilization need additional data; those sections explain what’s missing.</p>
      </section>

      <figure class="guide-screenshot guard-wide-screenshot">
        <img src="/images/cloud-cost-guard-billing-preview.jpg" width="1232" height="541" alt="An example of the uploaded-bill report, showing net cloud cost, positive charges, credits, and a breakdown by service" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <figcaption>A preview of the report using invented records. Your upload supplies your own figures.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <section aria-labelledby="next-title">
        <h2 id="next-title">Start with the biggest change.</h2>
        <p>With a comparison, open <strong>Findings</strong> and look at <strong>Biggest movement</strong> and <strong>Largest changes</strong>. Start with the service behind the largest increase. With one period, use <strong>Services</strong> to see which charges deserve a closer look.</p>
        <p>I’d ask the service owner what changed between the periods, then check usage, rates, and the underlying charges. Was there more demand? A different configuration? A price change? The service total gives us a place to investigate. It doesn’t answer those questions on its own.</p>
        <p>Keep the next step small: one service, someone who can explain it, and the records needed to check the explanation. A higher bill might make sense for the work being done.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="keep-title">
        <h2 id="keep-title">Ask a question. Keep the review.</h2>
        <p>Open <strong>Lumen</strong> and choose <strong>What changed?</strong> for a plain-language explanation. For uploaded bills, Lumen uses prepared answers from your report on your device, covering totals, services, credits, dates, and findings.</p>
        <p>Choose <strong>Printable report</strong> to download an HTML copy you can open in your browser. The CSV exports keep the normalized records, and <strong>Evidence JSON</strong> keeps the fuller report data. Save what you need before leaving.</p>
        <p>Want a quick preview before finding your export? The optional <strong>Try an example</strong> button on the start screen opens a sample dashboard.</p>
        <p>Your uploaded records, results, and Lumen questions stay in this tab’s memory. Reloading, closing the tab, or clearing the data removes them. The separate sample dashboard’s AI chat has its own disclosure about sending messages to an AI service.</p>
      </section>

      <footer class="guide-next">
        <h2>Give the change a next step.</h2>
        <p>Use the tool to find where the spending moved, then check the explanation with the person who knows the service. If you’d like help reviewing the records, usage, and terms together, that’s what a Technology Cost Review is for.</p>
        <div class="guide-actions">
          <a class="guide-button" href="https://guard.cloudandcapital.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-analytics-event="cta_click" data-analytics-destination="cloud_cost_guard" data-analytics-source="writing_archive"><ActionLabel label="Review your cloud bill" /></a>
          <a href="/services#technology-cost-review" data-analytics-event="cta_click" data-analytics-destination="services" data-analytics-source="writing_archive"><ActionLabel label="Explore a cost review" /></a>
        </div>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  .guard-guide h1 { max-width:none; }
  .guard-guide h1 > :is(span,em) { display:block; }
  .guard-guide h1 em { color:var(--clay-ink); font-style:italic; }
  .guard-guide header > p + p { margin-top:.8rem; }
  .guard-guide .guide-byline { margin-bottom:1rem; }
  .guard-guide .guide-button { color:white; background:var(--brand-green); border-color:var(--brand-green); }
  .guard-guide .guide-button :global(.action-label) { color:inherit; }
  .guard-guide .guide-screenshot img { padding:0; border:1px solid #d3c9c0; border-radius:12px; background:var(--cream); }
  .guard-providers { margin-block:1.5rem; border-top:1px solid #c8d1c0; }
  .guard-providers > div { display:grid; grid-template-columns:8rem minmax(0,1fr); gap:1.25rem; padding:1.25rem 0; border-bottom:1px solid #c8d1c0; }
  .guard-providers dt { color:var(--brand-green); font-size:1.2rem; font-weight:500; }
  .guard-providers dd { margin:0; line-height:1.75; text-wrap:pretty; }
  .guard-steps { padding-left:1.4rem; }
  .guard-file-help { margin-top:1.5rem; padding:1rem 1.3rem; border:1px solid #c8d1c0; border-radius:12px; }
  .guard-file-help summary { min-height:44px; padding-block:.5rem; cursor:pointer; font-weight:500; }
  .guard-file-help summary:focus-visible { outline:2px solid var(--clay-ink); outline-offset:4px; }
  .guard-file-help li { overflow-wrap:anywhere; }
  @media(max-width:760px) { .guard-wide-screenshot { display:none; } .guard-providers > div { grid-template-columns:1fr; gap:.35rem; } .guard-file-help { padding:.8rem 1rem; } }
</style>
